CAT = "cpafarLongLived"; SEG = "LLA"
Q = [
("A machine's price is $100,000. Sales tax is $6,000, freight $2,000 and installation $4,000. Repairing damage caused while installing it cost $1,500. The machine's capitalized cost is:",
 "$112,000", ["$113,500", "$106,000", "$100,000"],
 "Cost includes everything needed to get the asset ready for use: $100,000 + $6,000 + $2,000 + $4,000 = $112,000. Repairing avoidable damage is an expense, not a cost of the asset."),
("A company buys land with an old building for $300,000, demolishes the building for $20,000 and sells the salvage for $3,000. Title fees are $2,000, and architect fees for the new building are $15,000. The land's cost is:",
 "$319,000", ["$322,000", "$334,000", "$300,000"],
 "Land = $300,000 + $20,000 - $3,000 + $2,000 = $319,000. Net demolition cost belongs to the land; the architect's fees belong to the new building."),
("Equipment costing $90,000 with a $6,000 salvage value and a 7-year life is bought on April 1. Straight-line depreciation for the calendar year of purchase is:",
 "$9,000", ["$12,000", "$9,643", "$3,000"],
 "Annual depreciation is ($90,000 - $6,000) / 7 = $12,000, and 9 months of it is $9,000. Ignoring salvage gives $9,643."),
("An asset costs $50,000, has a $5,000 salvage value and a 5-year life. Using double-declining balance, depreciation in year 2 is:",
 "$12,000", ["$20,000", "$10,800", "$9,000"],
 "The rate is 40%. Year 1 is $50,000 x 40% = $20,000; year 2 is ($50,000 - $20,000) x 40% = $12,000. Salvage is ignored until book value nears it."),
("A machine costs $400,000, has a $40,000 salvage value and is expected to produce 900,000 units. It produces 120,000 units this year. Units-of-production depreciation is:",
 "$48,000", ["$53,333", "$40,000", "$36,000"],
 "The rate is ($400,000 - $40,000) / 900,000 = $0.40 per unit, and 120,000 x $0.40 = $48,000. Leaving out salvage gives $53,333."),
("Equipment that cost $70,000 with accumulated depreciation of $52,000 is sold for $21,000. The company records:",
 "A $3,000 gain", ["A $3,000 loss", "A $21,000 gain", "An $18,000 loss"],
 "Carrying amount is $70,000 - $52,000 = $18,000. Proceeds of $21,000 exceed it by $3,000, a gain."),
("An asset group held for use has a carrying amount of $500,000, undiscounted future cash flows of $520,000 and a fair value of $430,000. The impairment loss is:",
 "$0", ["$70,000", "$90,000", "$20,000"],
 "Under ASC 360 the carrying amount is first compared with undiscounted cash flows. $520,000 exceeds $500,000, so the asset is recoverable and no loss is recognized, even though fair value is lower."),
("An asset held for use has a carrying amount of $800,000, undiscounted future cash flows of $700,000 and a fair value of $610,000. The impairment loss is:",
 "$190,000", ["$100,000", "$90,000", "$0"],
 "The carrying amount exceeds undiscounted cash flows, so the asset is not recoverable. The loss is carrying amount minus fair value: $800,000 - $610,000 = $190,000."),
("After an impairment loss on equipment held for use, the equipment's fair value recovers. Reversing the loss is:",
 "Not permitted", ["Permitted up to original cost", "Required if value recovers", "Permitted through OCI"],
 "Under U.S. GAAP the written-down amount becomes the new cost basis for an asset held for use. Restoring a previously recognized impairment loss is prohibited."),
("A building classified as held for sale has a carrying amount of $260,000, a fair value of $250,000 and costs to sell of $15,000. The loss to recognize is:",
 "$25,000", ["$10,000", "$15,000", "$0"],
 "Held-for-sale assets are measured at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell: $250,000 - $15,000 = $235,000, so the loss is $260,000 - $235,000 = $25,000."),
("Which condition is required before a long-lived asset is classified as held for sale?",
 "Its sale within a year is probable", ["It is fully depreciated", "A buyer has paid a deposit", "It has been idle for six months"],
 "ASC 360 criteria include a committed plan, availability for immediate sale, an active buyer search, a sale probable within one year, a reasonable price and an unlikely change to the plan."),
("Once a long-lived asset is classified as held for sale, depreciation is:",
 "Stopped while it is held for sale", ["Continued on the same schedule", "Sped up to the sale date", "Switched to units of output"],
 "An asset held for sale is measured at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell, and it is not depreciated or amortized."),
("A major overhaul extends a delivery truck's useful life by three years. The cost of the overhaul is:",
 "Capitalized as part of the asset", ["Expensed as ordinary repairs", "Deferred as a prepaid asset", "Charged to retained earnings"],
 "Costs that extend useful life or increase capacity add to the asset. Routine repairs that only maintain it are expensed."),
("Accumulated depreciation was $400,000 at the start of the year. Depreciation expense was $110,000, and assets sold had $90,000 of accumulated depreciation. Ending accumulated depreciation is:",
 "$420,000", ["$600,000", "$510,000", "$380,000"],
 "$400,000 + $110,000 - $90,000 = $420,000. Adding the disposals instead of removing them gives $600,000."),
("Which cost of an internally developed patent is capitalized?",
 "Legal fees to register the patent", ["Lab salaries for the research", "Prototype building costs in research", "Materials used in research"],
 "Research and development costs are expensed as incurred under ASC 730. Legal and registration fees to obtain the patent are capitalized."),
("A patent bought on January 1 for $180,000 has 15 years of legal life left, but the company expects to benefit from it for 9 years. Annual amortization is:",
 "$20,000", ["$12,000", "$9,000", "$18,000"],
 "A finite-lived intangible is amortized over the shorter of its legal life and useful life: $180,000 / 9 = $20,000. Using the legal life gives $12,000."),
("A purchased trademark can be renewed indefinitely at little cost, and the company plans to keep using it. The trademark is:",
 "Not amortized", ["Amortized over 40 years", "Amortized over its legal term", "Expensed when acquired"],
 "An intangible with an indefinite useful life is not amortized. It is reviewed for impairment at least annually and more often if events suggest a decline."),
("Impairment of a finite-lived intangible asset is measured using:",
 "Recoverability, then fair value", ["Fair value only, in one step", "The reporting unit's goodwill", "No impairment review at all"],
 "Finite-lived intangibles follow the ASC 360 model for long-lived assets: an undiscounted cash flow recoverability step, then a loss measured as carrying amount minus fair value."),
("Under ASC 350-40, which cost of developing internal-use software is capitalized?",
 "Coding in the development stage", ["Training staff on the new system", "Evaluating vendors before approval", "Routine maintenance after launch"],
 "Costs in the application development stage, such as coding and installation, are capitalized. Preliminary project costs, training and maintenance are expensed."),
("Capitalized implementation costs of a cloud computing arrangement that is a service contract are expensed over:",
 "The term of the hosting arrangement", ["The period when they are incurred", "A fixed 40-year period", "The vendor's software life"],
 "ASU 2018-15 has customers capitalize these costs like internal-use software and expense them over the term of the hosting arrangement, in the same line as the hosting fees."),
("A company holds a 5% stake in a listed company's shares, with no significant influence. The shares are measured at:",
 "Fair value, changes in net income", ["Fair value, changes in OCI", "Amortized cost, with no fair value", "Cost less any impairment"],
 "Under ASC 321, equity securities with readily determinable fair values are measured at fair value through net income. The OCI category no longer exists for equity securities."),
("An available-for-sale debt security bought for $100,000 is worth $108,000 at year-end. The company records:",
 "An $8,000 gain in OCI", ["An $8,000 gain in net income", "No entry until it is sold", "An $8,000 deferred credit"],
 "Unrealized gains and losses on available-for-sale debt securities go to other comprehensive income. Trading securities would put the gain in net income."),
("A debt security may be classified as held to maturity only if the company has:",
 "The intent and ability to hold it", ["A security with a fixed maturity", "A bond bought at a discount", "A security with stated dividends"],
 "Held-to-maturity classification requires the positive intent and ability to hold the security to maturity. Equity securities can never be held to maturity."),
("A company buys a $100,000 bond with a 6% annual coupon for $103,000, a 5% effective yield, and holds it to maturity. Its amortized cost after the first year is:",
 "$102,150", ["$102,400", "$103,000", "$103,850"],
 "Interest income is $103,000 x 5% = $5,150 and cash received is $6,000, so the premium falls by $850 to $102,150."),
("Absent other evidence, significant influence and the equity method are presumed when an investor holds:",
 "20% to 50% of the voting stock", ["10% to 20% of the voting stock", "More than 50% of voting stock", "Any stake with a board seat"],
 "ASC 323 presumes significant influence at 20% or more of the voting stock. Above 50% the investor usually has control and consolidates."),
("An investor buys 30% of a company for $600,000, equal to book value. The company earns $250,000 and pays $100,000 in dividends. The investment's year-end carrying amount is:",
 "$645,000", ["$675,000", "$570,000", "$600,000"],
 "$600,000 + 30% x $250,000 - 30% x $100,000 = $600,000 + $75,000 - $30,000 = $645,000. Treating dividends as income gives $675,000."),
("Under the equity method, cash dividends received from the investee are recorded as:",
 "A reduction of the investment", ["Dividend income", "Other comprehensive income", "An increase in the investment"],
 "The investor already recognized its share of earnings, so dividends are a return of investment and reduce the carrying amount."),
("An equity security without a readily determinable fair value may be measured using the measurement alternative, which is:",
 "Cost, adjusted for observable prices", ["Amortized cost with interest", "Fair value through OCI", "The equity method, by ownership share"],
 "ASC 321 allows cost minus impairment, plus or minus changes from observable price changes in orderly transactions for the same or a similar security."),
]
